insert into public.flashcards (id, cert, deck, domain, objective, front, back) values
('cy-f-200','cysa','core',1,'1.2',$$What does abnormal CPU or memory consumption on a host suggest to an analyst?$$,$$Unexplained, sustained resource consumption - not tied to a scheduled job or known traffic increase - can indicate cryptomining malware, a runaway process from an exploit, or another compromise consuming the host's resources for an attacker's benefit.$$),
('cy-f-201','cysa','core',1,'1.2',$$What does a host's free disk space rapidly disappearing indicate as a security signal?$$,$$Rapid, unexplained capacity consumption can mean ransomware writing encrypted copies alongside originals, an attacker staging files for exfiltration, or log flooding used to mask other activity - all capacity-consumption indicators worth investigating.$$),
('cy-f-202','cysa','core',1,'1.2',$$Why is an unexpected new scheduled task or cron job a malicious-activity indicator?$$,$$Attackers use scheduled tasks and cron jobs as a persistence mechanism, re-launching their payload after a reboot or logoff; an unfamiliar task invoking a script from a temp directory or running with elevated rights warrants review.$$),
('cy-f-203','cysa','core',1,'1.2',$$What is a rogue access point and why is it a network indicator of compromise?$$,$$An unauthorized wireless access point - planted by an attacker or set up unknowingly by an employee - that bypasses the organization's monitored network controls, potentially giving an attacker a foothold or an easy path for data to leave unmonitored.$$),
('cy-f-204','cysa','core',1,'1.2',$$What does irregular peer-to-peer (host-to-host) communication indicate in an enterprise network?$$,$$Workstations that normally talk only to central servers suddenly communicating directly with each other, especially across multiple pairs, is irregular for a client-server architecture and is a recognized indicator of lateral movement or worm-like spreading.$$),
('cy-f-205','cysa','core',1,'1.2',$$Why is a productivity application spawning a scripting engine like PowerShell considered an indicator of malicious activity?$$,$$Applications such as Word or Excel have no legitimate reason to fork a shell or scripting engine during normal use; an anomalous parent-child process relationship like this, especially right after opening a document, is a classic sign of a malicious macro launching a hidden payload.$$),
('cy-f-206','cysa','core',1,'1.2',$$What is an unauthorized privilege escalation indicator?$$,$$A user or service account suddenly gaining rights or group membership it previously lacked - such as being added to a privileged administrative group - without an approved change request, signaling possible account compromise or insider misuse.$$),
('cy-f-207','cysa','core',1,'1.2',$$How do Windows Registry Run keys serve as a malicious-activity indicator?$$,$$Run/RunOnce registry keys launch their referenced program automatically at logon, making them a simple, favorite persistence location for malware; an unfamiliar entry pointing to a script in a temp folder is a common finding during triage.$$),
('cy-f-208','cysa','core',1,'1.2',$$Why are social engineering cues treated as indicators of malicious activity alongside technical artifacts?$$,$$Many intrusions begin with a human-targeted pretext rather than a technical exploit; manufactured urgency, impersonated authority, and pressure to skip verification are themselves an indicator category analysts must recognize, distinct from network or host artifacts.$$),
('cy-f-209','cysa','core',1,'1.3',$$What does following a TCP stream in a packet-analysis tool let an analyst do?$$,$$Reconstructs an entire conversation between two hosts into one readable, ordered exchange - such as a full HTTP request/response or a plaintext credential exchange - instead of interpreting disjointed individual packets.$$),
('cy-f-210','cysa','core',1,'1.3',$$What is hash-based malware identification and its main limitation?$$,$$Computing a file's cryptographic hash and checking it against known-malware databases for a fast, exact-match identification; the limitation is that even a trivial change to the file produces a completely different hash, so it misses variants and repacked samples.$$),
('cy-f-211','cysa','core',1,'1.3',$$What is the difference between static and dynamic malware analysis?$$,$$Static analysis examines a file's code and structure without executing it (strings, headers, disassembly); dynamic analysis actually runs the file, usually in a sandbox, and observes its real behavior - each catches things the other can miss.$$),
('cy-f-212','cysa','core',1,'1.3',$$What does strings analysis reveal when examining a suspicious binary?$$,$$Extracting readable ASCII/Unicode text embedded in a binary can surface hardcoded C2 domains, file paths, error messages, or encoded commands, giving useful leads without executing the file or fully reverse-engineering it.$$),
('cy-f-213','cysa','core',1,'1.3',$$What can memory forensics reveal that disk-based scanning misses?$$,$$Analyzing a RAM capture can uncover injected code, decrypted payloads, and fileless malware that exists only in memory and never touches disk, so a static disk scan has nothing to detect.$$),
('cy-f-214','cysa','core',1,'1.3',$$What is a YARA rule used for?$$,$$A pattern-matching rule defining strings, byte sequences, or conditions that classify files as belonging to a known malware family, letting analysts scan large file sets or live systems for matches at scale.$$),
('cy-f-215','cysa','core',1,'1.3',$$What is the operational difference between an IDS and an IPS?$$,$$An IDS passively monitors and alerts on suspicious traffic without touching it; an IPS sits inline and can actively block or drop malicious traffic in real time, at the cost of becoming a potential single point of failure if it misbehaves.$$),
('cy-f-216','cysa','core',1,'1.3',$$How does command-line auditing (Sysmon, Event ID 4688) help detect malicious activity?$$,$$It logs the full command-line arguments used to launch a process, exposing obfuscated or base64-encoded PowerShell and other living-off-the-land abuse that process-name-only logging would miss.$$),
('cy-f-217','cysa','core',1,'1.3',$$What role do IP/domain/URL reputation lookups play in triage?$$,$$Quickly checking an indicator against a reputation service flags known-bad infrastructure and adds context to an alert, letting analysts prioritize investigation before doing a full manual analysis.$$),
('cy-f-218','cysa','core',1,'1.3',$$What is trend analysis in log/detection data used for?$$,$$Comparing current volumes or patterns - login attempts, DNS queries, alert counts - against an established baseline over time, surfacing gradual or aggregate deviations that no single snapshot or threshold alert would catch.$$),
('cy-f-219','cysa','core',1,'1.3',$$How do connection-log tools like Zeek differ from full packet capture for network analysis?$$,$$They produce structured, searchable metadata records for every connection, similar to an enriched NetFlow, at a fraction of the storage cost of full packet capture - the trade-off is no retained payload for deep content inspection.$$),
('cy-f-220','cysa','core',1,'1.4',$$What characterizes an Advanced Persistent Threat (APT)?$$,$$A well-resourced, often nation-state-linked actor conducting stealthy, long-term intrusions focused on sustained access and a specific strategic objective, rather than a single opportunistic payoff.$$),
('cy-f-221','cysa','core',1,'1.4',$$How do organized crime and hacktivist threat actors typically differ in motivation?$$,$$Organized crime actors are financially motivated - ransomware, fraud, reselling stolen data; hacktivists are ideologically motivated, seeking to embarrass, disrupt, or publicize a cause rather than profit directly.$$),
('cy-f-222','cysa','core',1,'1.4',$$How does the Traffic Light Protocol (TLP) control the redistribution of shared threat intelligence?$$,$$Senders label intelligence RED (named recipients only, no further sharing), AMBER (limited to the recipient's organization on a need-to-know basis), GREEN (shareable community-wide), or WHITE/CLEAR (unlimited distribution), so recipients know exactly how far they may pass it on.$$),
('cy-f-223','cysa','core',1,'1.4',$$What is the purpose of an ISAC (Information Sharing and Analysis Center)?$$,$$A sector-specific community - financial services, healthcare, energy, and similar - where member organizations share threat intelligence, indicators, and best practices relevant to threats targeting their industry.$$),
('cy-f-224','cysa','core',1,'1.4',$$What is MISP and what does it provide a threat-intel team?$$,$$An open-source Malware Information Sharing Platform - a structured database and sharing hub for storing, correlating, and exchanging indicators and threat events, often feeding SIEM/EDR tooling automatically.$$),
('cy-f-225','cysa','core',1,'1.4',$$What do the Admiralty/NATO source reliability and information credibility scales rate separately?$$,$$Source reliability (graded A-F, how trustworthy the source has been historically) and information credibility (graded 1-6, how plausible this specific report is) are scored independently, since even a reliable source can pass along unconfirmed information.$$),
('cy-f-226','cysa','core',1,'1.4',$$Beyond strategic and tactical, what do operational and technical threat intelligence each cover?$$,$$Operational intelligence details a specific, often imminent campaign or planned attack against a target; technical intelligence is narrow, machine-readable data - specific indicators or malware signatures - consumed directly by detection tools.$$),
('cy-f-227','cysa','core',1,'1.4',$$What is the Hunting Maturity Model (HMM) used to assess?$$,$$An organization's threat-hunting sophistication, ranging from HM0 (relying entirely on automated alerts) up to HM4 (leading - highly automated, with most hunting procedures turned into permanent detections that feed back into the program).$$),
('cy-f-228','cysa','core',1,'1.4',$$What distinguishes hypothesis-driven hunting from intel-driven hunting?$$,$$Hypothesis-driven hunts test an analyst's own testable theory about possible compromise; intel-driven hunts start from external threat intelligence - such as a new report on an actor's TTPs - and search the environment for those specific behaviors.$$),
('cy-f-229','cysa','core',1,'1.4',$$What is situational-awareness-driven (crown-jewel-based) threat hunting?$$,$$Hunting focused on an organization's most critical assets and business risks rather than a specific actor's TTPs - proactively watching what matters most regardless of which threat might target it.$$),
('cy-f-230','cysa','core',1,'1.4',$$What is crown jewel analysis and how does it inform intelligence priorities?$$,$$Identifying which systems, data, and processes are most critical to the organization, so that threat-intelligence collection, monitoring, and hunting effort concentrates where compromise would cause the most damage.$$),
('cy-f-231','cysa','core',1,'1.4',$$What is adversary emulation (purple teaming) in a threat-intelligence-driven exercise?$$,$$A red team deliberately replicates a specific threat actor's known TTPs, drawn from ATT&CK or intel reporting, while the blue team validates and tunes detections in real time - testing actual coverage against a real actor's behavior rather than a generic attack simulation.$$),
('cy-f-232','cysa','core',1,'1.4',$$What does the ATT&CK Navigator help a threat-intel or detection team visualize?$$,$$A heat-map style view of ATT&CK techniques showing which ones an organization has detection coverage for, which have been exercised, and which gaps remain - guiding where to invest next.$$),
('cy-f-233','cysa','core',1,'1.4',$$Why do indicators of compromise (IOCs) decay or expire over time?$$,$$Attackers rotate infrastructure - IPs, domains, hashes - frequently and cheaply, so an IOC list not continuously refreshed against current feeds grows stale and stops matching current activity, part of why the pyramid of pain favors TTP-based detection.$$),
('cy-f-234','cysa','core',1,'1.4',$$What is dark web monitoring as a threat-intelligence source?$$,$$Tracking criminal marketplaces, forums, and paste sites for mentions of the organization, leaked credentials, or the sale of stolen data - providing early warning of a breach or a planned attack.$$),
('cy-f-235','cysa','core',1,'1.5',$$What is the difference between automation and orchestration in SOC operations?$$,$$Automation performs one repetitive task without human input, such as auto-blocking a confirmed malicious hash; orchestration coordinates multiple tools and automated steps into a single end-to-end workflow - enrich, contain, notify, ticket.$$),
('cy-f-236','cysa','core',1,'1.5',$$What is the difference between a runbook and a playbook in incident handling?$$,$$A runbook is a fixed, linear, step-by-step procedure for a specific routine task; a playbook is a decision-tree-style response guide covering branching scenarios for a broader incident type, often partially executed through SOAR.$$),
('cy-f-237','cysa','core',1,'1.5',$$Why do Standard Operating Procedures (SOPs) improve SOC efficiency?$$,$$They standardize exactly how a routine task should be performed regardless of which analyst is on shift, reducing inconsistent handling, cutting training time, and lowering the chance a step gets skipped under pressure.$$),
('cy-f-238','cysa','core',1,'1.5',$$How does an API enable automation between security tools?$$,$$It lets one tool programmatically query or command another - such as a SOAR platform pulling enrichment data from a threat-intel API or instructing an EDR to isolate a host - without an analyst manually switching between consoles.$$),
('cy-f-239','cysa','core',1,'1.5',$$What is a webhook and how is it used in security automation?$$,$$An event-driven callback where one system automatically pushes data to a listening endpoint the instant something happens, such as a new alert firing, triggering a downstream workflow immediately instead of waiting on a scheduled poll.$$),
('cy-f-240','cysa','core',1,'1.5',$$What is a "single pane of glass" in security operations?$$,$$A unified dashboard that aggregates data and alerts from multiple underlying tools into one view, reducing the time analysts spend switching between separate consoles during triage.$$),
('cy-f-241','cysa','core',1,'1.5',$$What is automated data enrichment in alert triage?$$,$$Automatically appending context - WHOIS data, geolocation, a reputation score, the owning asset's business criticality - to a raw alert before it reaches an analyst, so triage starts with answers instead of manual lookups.$$),
('cy-f-242','cysa','core',1,'1.5',$$What is automated IOC extraction?$$,$$Using scripts or tooling to automatically pull indicators - hashes, IPs, domains - out of threat reports or malware samples and load them into detection feeds, instead of an analyst manually copying them.$$),
('cy-f-243','cysa','core',1,'1.5',$$Why do SOCs automate threat feed aggregation?$$,$$Combining multiple feeds automatically, deduplicating overlapping indicators, and normalizing inconsistent formats produces one clean, actionable feed - manually reconciling several raw feeds by hand doesn't scale.$$),
('cy-f-244','cysa','core',1,'1.5',$$What is automated malware signature creation?$$,$$A pipeline that detonates a sample in a sandbox and automatically generates a detection signature from the observed behavior or code patterns, spreading detection to related systems far faster than manual rule-writing.$$),
('cy-f-245','cysa','core',1,'1.5',$$How does machine learning improve SOC efficiency without replacing analysts?$$,$$ML models baseline normal behavior and surface genuinely unusual events at a scale humans can't match manually, cutting the volume of low-value alerts analysts must triage by hand.$$),
('cy-f-246','cysa','core',1,'1.5',$$Why does "minimizing human engagement" matter as an efficiency goal, and what is NOT meant by it?$$,$$It means removing repetitive, low-judgment manual steps - copy-pasting indicators, manual ticket creation - so analysts spend time on judgment calls; it does not mean removing human oversight of consequential response actions.$$),
('cy-f-247','cysa','core',1,'1.5',$$What does SOAR case/ticket management integration provide?$$,$$Automatic creation, updating, and closing of incident tickets as a playbook executes, producing a documented record of response actions without an analyst manually logging every step.$$),
('cy-f-248','cysa','core',1,'1.5',$$What is a common technology-integration challenge that slows SOC automation efforts?$$,$$A legacy system with no API, or only a limited proprietary one, forces a manual step into an otherwise automated workflow, becoming the bottleneck that caps how much of a process can actually run unattended.$$),
('cy-f-249','cysa','core',1,'1.5',$$What is "span of control" in a SOC, and why does it matter for efficiency?$$,$$The number of alerts, systems, or cases one analyst can effectively manage at once; exceeding it causes rushed, lower-quality investigations even when the underlying tooling is otherwise efficient.$$),
('cy-f-250','cysa','core',1,'1.5',$$Why does clear service ownership matter for a playbook or automated process?$$,$$Without a named owner responsible for maintaining it, a playbook goes stale as tools and environments change, quietly failing or taking wrong actions until someone happens to notice.$$),
('cy-f-251','cysa','core',1,'1.5',$$How does a continuous-improvement feedback loop keep automation effective?$$,$$Lessons learned from real incidents and near-misses are fed back into updating playbooks and automation logic, rather than leaving them as static documents that drift out of date.$$),
('cy-f-252','cysa','core',1,'1.5',$$What metrics are commonly used to show automation/efficiency gains?$$,$$Analyst hours saved, mean time to respond (MTTR) before and after automation, and reductions in alert-to-ticket or alert-to-response time - concrete numbers that justify continued investment.$$),
('cy-f-253','cysa','core',1,'1.5',$$What is alert correlation/deduplication automation and why does it matter?$$,$$Automatically grouping multiple related alerts - one attack tripping several separate rules - into a single case instead of several duplicate tickets, cutting noise and preventing analysts from investigating the same incident repeatedly.$$),
('cy-f-254','cysa','core',1,'1.5',$$What risk does unrestricted auto-remediation introduce, and how is it managed?$$,$$An automated action, such as auto-blocking an IP or disabling an account, can misfire on a false positive and cause a business outage; guardrails like approval gates for high-impact actions and staged rollouts limit that blast radius.$$),
('cy-a-100','cysa','acronym',1,'1.2',$$FIM$$,$$File Integrity Monitoring - detects unauthorized changes to critical files or configurations by comparing current hashes against a known-good baseline.$$),
('cy-a-101','cysa','acronym',1,'1.2',$$RAT$$,$$Remote Access Trojan - malware that gives an attacker hidden, persistent remote control of a compromised host.$$),
('cy-a-102','cysa','acronym',1,'1.2',$$LOLBin$$,$$Living Off the Land Binary - a legitimate, pre-installed operating system executable (e.g., certutil, mshta) abused by attackers to carry out malicious actions while evading file-based detection.$$),
('cy-a-103','cysa','acronym',1,'1.3',$$IDS$$,$$Intrusion Detection System - passively monitors traffic or hosts and alerts on suspicious activity without blocking it.$$),
('cy-a-104','cysa','acronym',1,'1.3',$$IPS$$,$$Intrusion Prevention System - sits inline with traffic and can actively block or drop malicious activity in real time.$$),
('cy-a-105','cysa','acronym',1,'1.3',$$YARA$$,$$A pattern-matching rule language ("Yet Another Recursive Acronym") used to identify and classify malware samples by strings, byte sequences, and conditions.$$),
('cy-a-106','cysa','acronym',1,'1.3',$$NTA$$,$$Network Traffic Analysis - inspecting network communications (flows, connection metadata, or content) to detect anomalies and malicious behavior.$$),
('cy-a-107','cysa','acronym',1,'1.4',$$TLP$$,$$Traffic Light Protocol - a labeling scheme (RED/AMBER/GREEN/WHITE) that tells recipients how widely shared threat intelligence may be redistributed.$$),
('cy-a-108','cysa','acronym',1,'1.4',$$ISAC$$,$$Information Sharing and Analysis Center - a sector-specific community where organizations share threat intelligence and best practices relevant to their industry.$$),
('cy-a-109','cysa','acronym',1,'1.4',$$MISP$$,$$Malware Information Sharing Platform - an open-source platform for storing, correlating, and sharing threat indicators and events between organizations.$$),
('cy-a-110','cysa','acronym',1,'1.4',$$APT$$,$$Advanced Persistent Threat - a well-resourced, often state-linked actor conducting stealthy, long-term intrusions to maintain sustained access.$$),
('cy-a-111','cysa','acronym',1,'1.5',$$SOP$$,$$Standard Operating Procedure - a documented, standardized set of steps for performing a routine task consistently regardless of who performs it.$$),
('cy-a-112','cysa','acronym',1,'1.5',$$API$$,$$Application Programming Interface - a defined way for one program to programmatically query or control another, the backbone of tool-to-tool security automation.$$),
('cy-a-113','cysa','acronym',1,'1.5',$$RPA$$,$$Robotic Process Automation - software "bots" that mimic repetitive manual user actions, like clicking and copying data between systems, to automate tasks that lack a direct API.$$),
('cy-y-050','cysa','feynman',1,'1.2',$$Explain to a junior analyst why a spike in CPU usage on a server can be a security indicator, not just a performance problem.$$,$$Model answer: performance issues do happen for benign reasons, like a batch job or a busy sale. But if a spike is unexplained - no scheduled job, no traffic increase, constant rather than following the normal daily pattern - treat it as a potential indicator: cryptomining malware, a runaway process from an exploit, or a scanning/brute-force script can all pin the CPU. The lesson is to check why before dismissing it, and correlate with what's actually supposed to be running.$$),
('cy-y-051','cysa','feynman',2,'1.3',$$Explain how you would safely determine whether a suspicious email attachment is malicious, using hashing and sandboxing together.$$,$$Model answer: first hash the file and check it against reputation/threat-intel databases - if it matches known malware, you're done cheaply. If it's unknown, don't run it on a real machine; detonate it in an isolated sandbox and watch what it actually does: files it drops, registry changes, network callbacks. The combination is efficient, since a fast hash lookup catches known threats, and safe, since anything unknown is only ever executed in a disposable, monitored environment.$$),
('cy-y-052','cysa','feynman',2,'1.4',$$Explain how you would decide whether to label a threat report TLP:GREEN or TLP:AMBER before sharing it with a partner ISAC.$$,$$Model answer: ask what happens if this leaves your control entirely. If the report only describes general TTPs with no sensitive internal detail, and the wider trusted community benefits from knowing it, GREEN is appropriate since it can spread across the community. If it includes something that identifies your organization specifically, or context you only want the immediate recipient's organization to use internally, that calls for AMBER, restricting it to their organization on a need-to-know basis. The label controls the blast radius of the information itself, not how important it is.$$),
('cy-y-053','cysa','feynman',3,'1.4',$$Explain the Hunting Maturity Model using an analogy a new hunt-team lead would understand.$$,$$Model answer: think of a fire department's evolution. At the lowest level, they only respond when a smoke alarm (an automated alert) goes off - purely reactive. At the middle levels, they start doing scheduled inspections for hazards using standard checklists (procedural hunts). At the highest level, they've automated so much of what they learn from each inspection into the smoke-alarm system itself that new hazard types get caught automatically next time, and the team spends its time on genuinely novel cases the automated system can't yet catch. Maturity isn't about hunting more, it's about turning what you learn from hunting into permanent, automated detection.$$),
('cy-y-054','cysa','feynman',2,'1.5',$$Explain automation versus orchestration to someone who thinks they are the same thing.$$,$$Model answer: automation is one gear turning by itself - a script that automatically blocks a malicious hash the moment it's seen. Orchestration is the whole gearbox: it decides which gears turn, in what order, and connects them into one outcome - enrich the alert, confirm the hash is truly malicious, block it, open a ticket, notify the analyst. A SOAR platform's value is mostly orchestration; automation is just one gear inside it.$$),
('cy-y-055','cysa','feynman',2,'1.5',$$Explain, using a realistic failure scenario, why fully unattended auto-remediation is risky.$$,$$Model answer: imagine a detection rule misfires on a false positive - a legitimate software update server gets flagged as command-and-control because of a new pattern - and an automated playbook instantly blocks its IP org-wide. Now a business-critical update process breaks silently, and nobody finds out until people start asking why updates stopped. That's the blast-radius risk: automation executes exactly what it's told, at machine speed, with no judgment about whether the trigger was actually right. The fix isn't to avoid automation, it's to add an approval gate or staged rollout for any action with real consequences.$$),
('cy-y-056','cysa','feynman',1,'1.5',$$Explain why writing down a playbook doesn't help if nobody owns keeping it updated.$$,$$Model answer: a playbook is a snapshot of how to respond given the tools and environment at the time it was written. Tools get replaced, IP ranges change, new steps become necessary - and if no one is assigned to revisit and update it, the playbook quietly goes stale. An analyst following it during a real incident may take a step that no longer applies, wasting time or making things worse. Ownership isn't paperwork, it's what keeps the document trustworthy months later.$$),
('cy-y-057','cysa','feynman',2,'1.5',$$Explain how an automated data-enrichment pipeline changes what an analyst sees when a new alert appears.$$,$$Model answer: without enrichment, an alert is just a bare connection to some external IP, and the analyst has to manually look up who owns it, where it's located, whether it's known-bad, and who owns the internal asset involved - several lookups before real analysis even starts. With automated enrichment, all of that context is already attached the moment the alert lands: reputation score, geolocation, WHOIS, asset owner. The analyst's first look is already an informed one, so triage time shrinks dramatically.$$);
insert into public.questions (id, cert, domain, objective, qtype, difficulty, stem, choices, answer, explanation) values
('cy-q-200','cysa',1,'1.2','mcq',2,$q$A finance department workstation's CPU sits pegged at 100% around the clock, even overnight when the user is logged off and no scheduled job is configured to run. What should the analyst suspect FIRST?$q$,$q$["A hardware fan failure","Cryptomining malware consuming CPU cycles for an attacker's profit","A normal Windows Update check","A printer driver conflict"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Sustained, unexplained CPU consumption with no legitimate scheduled process running - especially persisting while the user is away - is a classic sign of cryptomining malware silently using the host's resources. Hardware and driver issues don't typically produce constant full-load usage, and update checks are brief, not indefinite.$q$),
('cy-q-201','cysa',1,'1.2','mcq',2,$q$A file server's free disk space drops from 40% to under 2% over a few hours, with no scheduled backup running and no unusually large legitimate uploads reported. What is the MOST likely explanation an analyst should investigate?$q$,$q$["A user finally emptied their recycle bin","Ransomware creating encrypted copies of files alongside the originals, or an attacker staging data for exfiltration","A routine log rotation completing on schedule","The disk's reported capacity is simply inaccurate"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Rapid, unexplained capacity consumption is a host-based indicator commonly tied to ransomware, which writes an encrypted copy before removing the original, or to an attacker staging large volumes of data before exfiltrating it. Emptying a recycle bin frees space rather than consuming it, and neither log rotation nor a reporting glitch explains a drop this fast.$q$),
('cy-q-202','cysa',1,'1.2','mcq',1,$q$During triage, an analyst finds a scheduled task configured to run an unsigned script from a temp folder every time a workstation boots, and no one on the IT team recalls creating it. What does this MOST likely represent?$q$,$q$["A default operating system task","An attacker's persistence mechanism ensuring their payload restarts after reboot or logoff","A printer driver update routine","A benign leftover from software uninstalled months ago"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$An unrecognized scheduled task launching an unsigned script from a temp directory at every boot is a textbook persistence indicator - it guarantees the attacker's tooling survives a reboot without needing to re-compromise the host.$q$),
('cy-q-203','cysa',1,'1.2','mcq',1,$q$A wireless survey turns up an access point broadcasting the corporate SSID from a MAC address not listed in the wireless controller's managed inventory, located in a supply closet. What does this represent?$q$,$q$["A guest network intentionally provisioned by IT","A rogue access point that could give an attacker a foothold bypassing normal network controls","A firmware update artifact","A misconfigured but authorized company router"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$An unmanaged access point mimicking the corporate SSID from an unexpected location is a rogue AP - whether planted by an attacker or set up by an unaware employee, it bypasses the organization's monitored wireless security controls and is a real risk.$q$),
('cy-q-204','cysa',1,'1.2','multi',3,$q$In an environment where workstations are architected to talk only to central servers, an analyst observes several ordinary user workstations exchanging traffic directly with each other on a nonstandard port overnight. Select the TWO conclusions supported by this observation. (Choose two.)$q$,$q$["This pattern is consistent with lateral movement or worm-like spreading between hosts","This traffic pattern is irregular for the network's client-server architecture and warrants investigation","This is expected behavior for any modern corporate network","The nonstandard port guarantees the traffic is encrypted and therefore safe"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1]$q$::jsonb,$q$Direct host-to-host communication in a network designed around client-server flows, repeated across multiple pairs on a nonstandard port, is irregular peer-to-peer traffic - a recognized indicator of lateral movement or self-propagating malware. It is not expected baseline behavior, and using a nonstandard port says nothing about whether traffic is encrypted or benign.$q$),
('cy-q-205','cysa',1,'1.2','mcq',3,$q$EDR telemetry shows a word-processing application spawning a scripting-engine process with an encoded command-line argument, immediately after a user opened an email attachment. What does this process relationship represent?$q$,$q$["A normal feature used for spell-check","An abnormal parent-child process relationship consistent with a malicious macro launching a payload","A scheduled application update","A benign printer spooler interaction"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$A word processor has no legitimate reason to spawn a scripting engine, let alone with an encoded argument. This parent-child relationship, especially right after opening an attachment, is a strong indicator that a malicious macro executed a hidden payload.$q$),
('cy-q-206','cysa',1,'1.2','mcq',2,$q$A service account with no documented change request suddenly appears as a member of a highly privileged administrative group. What kind of indicator is this?$q$,$q$["A routine IT provisioning step","An unauthorized privilege escalation indicator suggesting the account may be compromised or misused","Evidence the account was deleted","A benign audit log rollover"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Privileges appearing without an approved change - especially an account gaining broad administrative rights - is a strong indicator of account compromise or insider misuse, and should trigger investigation rather than being assumed routine.$q$),
('cy-q-207','cysa',1,'1.2','mcq',2,$q$An analyst reviewing autorun locations finds an unfamiliar registry entry configured to launch at every logon, pointing to a script in a temp folder. What is the significance of this finding?$q$,$q$["Autorun registry keys have no security relevance","It is a common persistence technique, launching the referenced script automatically at every logon","It only affects application startup speed","It indicates a hardware driver issue"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Autorun registry keys execute their target automatically at logon, making them a favorite, simple persistence location for malware. An unfamiliar entry pointing to a temp-folder script is worth treating as a likely indicator of compromise, not a performance quirk.$q$),
('cy-q-208','cysa',1,'1.2','mcq',2,$q$An employee reports receiving an urgent email, apparently from the CEO, demanding an immediate wire transfer and warning against "checking with anyone first." Aside from any technical artifacts in the message, what indicator category does this pretext itself represent?$q$,$q$["A network-based indicator","A social engineering indicator - the use of urgency and false authority to bypass normal judgment and verification","An application log indicator","A host-based indicator"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Manufactured urgency combined with impersonated authority, and explicit pressure not to verify, is a social engineering indicator in its own right - a non-technical signal of malicious intent that analysts must recognize alongside artifacts like headers or hashes.$q$),
('cy-q-209','cysa',1,'1.2','matching',2,$q$Match each indicator category to an example that best fits it.$q$,$q${"left":["Network-related indicator","Host-related indicator","Application-related indicator","Other (non-technical) indicator"],"right":["Unexpected traffic to a rarely-used external port range","A new local administrator account appearing without a change ticket","A web application's error log showing repeated attempts hitting an internal-only endpoint","An email demanding urgent action while discouraging verification"]}$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1,2,3]$q$::jsonb,$q$Indicators of malicious activity span categories: network indicators involve traffic patterns, host indicators involve changes on an endpoint, application indicators appear in an app's own logs or behavior, and non-technical indicators like social-engineering pretexts fall outside the technical categories but are equally worth recognizing.$q$),
('cy-q-210','cysa',1,'1.3','mcq',1,$q$An analyst wants to read an entire plaintext HTTP conversation between a client and a server as one continuous exchange, rather than piecing it together packet by packet in Wireshark. Which feature should they use?$q$,$q$["Follow TCP Stream","Statistics > IO Graph","The Protocol Hierarchy filter","Export Objects > DICOM"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Follow TCP Stream reconstructs an entire conversation between two endpoints into one readable view, exactly the technique needed to read a full request/response exchange instead of interpreting individual packets.$q$),
('cy-q-211','cysa',1,'1.3','mcq',2,$q$An analyst computes the SHA-256 hash of a suspicious file and finds no match in a malware-intelligence database, even though the file behaves suspiciously in a sandbox. What is the MOST likely explanation?$q$,$q$["Hashing never produces false negatives","The file is a variant with even a minor change, producing a completely different hash than any known sample","SHA-256 cannot be used for malware identification","The sandbox result must be wrong"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Hash-based identification is exact-match only - changing even a single byte of a file produces an entirely different hash. A slightly modified or repacked variant of known malware will not match a hash database even though its behavior is malicious, which is exactly why hash lookups are paired with behavioral techniques like sandboxing.$q$),
('cy-q-212','cysa',1,'1.3','mcq',1,$q$Which statement correctly distinguishes static from dynamic malware analysis?$q$,$q$["Static analysis executes the file in a sandbox; dynamic analysis only reads its code","Static analysis examines the file's code and structure without running it; dynamic analysis observes its behavior by actually executing it","They are two names for the same technique","Dynamic analysis is always safer than static analysis"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Static analysis - strings, headers, disassembly - never executes the sample, while dynamic analysis runs it, typically in an isolated sandbox, to observe what it actually does. Each reveals things the other can miss, which is why both are used together.$q$),
('cy-q-213','cysa',1,'1.3','mcq',2,$q$Running a strings-extraction utility against an unknown executable reveals a hardcoded URL pointing to an unfamiliar domain, alongside readable debug file paths. What has this technique accomplished?$q$,$q$["Fully reverse-engineered the binary's logic","Surfaced potential indicators, such as a possible C2 domain, directly from embedded text without needing full disassembly","Executed the binary safely","Confirmed the file is benign"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Strings analysis extracts readable text embedded in a binary - often revealing hardcoded C2 domains, credentials, or debug paths - without executing the file or performing full reverse engineering. It's a fast first pass, not a complete verdict on the file's behavior.$q$),
('cy-q-214','cysa',1,'1.3','mcq',3,$q$A disk-based antivirus scan finds nothing on a host that EDR flagged for suspicious network beaconing. Which technique is MOST likely to reveal what static disk scanning missed?$q$,$q$["Rerunning the same disk scan with different settings","Memory forensics on a RAM capture, to find injected code or a fileless payload that never touched disk","Reviewing the printer queue","Reformatting the drive"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Fileless malware operates entirely in memory, leaving nothing for a disk scan to find. Capturing and analyzing RAM can reveal injected code, decrypted payloads, and running artifacts that never existed as a file on disk.$q$),
('cy-q-215','cysa',1,'1.3','mcq',1,$q$What is the primary purpose of writing a YARA rule?$q$,$q$["To encrypt network traffic","To define patterns - strings, byte sequences, or conditions - that classify files as belonging to a known malware family","To configure firewall access control lists","To schedule automated backups"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$YARA rules describe identifying patterns - specific strings, byte sequences, or combinations of conditions - that let an analyst or tool scan files and flag matches to a known malware family, at scale.$q$),
('cy-q-216','cysa',1,'1.3','mcq',2,$q$A security team wants a control that can automatically drop a known-malicious packet in real time before it reaches its target, rather than only alerting after the fact. Which control fits, and what is the associated trade-off?$q$,$q$["An IDS, which alerts without touching traffic, so nothing is blocked","An IPS, which sits inline and can block traffic in real time, but becomes a potential single point of failure if it misbehaves","A honeypot, which only observes attacker behavior","A vulnerability scanner, which finds weaknesses but takes no real-time action"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$An IPS operates inline and can actively block malicious traffic the moment it's seen, unlike a purely passive IDS. The trade-off is that, sitting inline, a misconfigured or malfunctioning IPS can itself disrupt legitimate traffic or become a chokepoint.$q$),
('cy-q-217','cysa',1,'1.3','mcq',1,$q$An attacker runs a base64-encoded PowerShell command specifically to avoid writing anything readable to disk. Which detection technique is MOST likely to expose this activity?$q$,$q$["Disk signature scanning alone","Command-line process auditing, such as Sysmon or Event ID 4688, that logs the full launch arguments of each process","Physical port security","Password complexity policy enforcement"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Encoded PowerShell commands leave nothing meaningful on disk for signature scanning to catch, but command-line auditing logs the actual arguments used to launch a process, exposing the encoded payload for decoding and analysis.$q$),
('cy-q-218','cysa',1,'1.3','multi',2,$q$Select the TWO legitimate uses of an IP/domain reputation lookup service during alert triage. (Choose two.)$q$,$q$["Quickly flagging whether an indicator is already known-bad, to prioritize investigation","Enriching an alert with context before an analyst manually investigates further","Serving as the sole, final determination of guilt with no further review","Guaranteeing that an indicator with no reputation history is definitely safe"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1]$q$::jsonb,$q$Reputation lookups are a fast triage aid: they flag known-bad indicators for priority and add useful context to an alert. They are not a final verdict - a clean reputation score only means an indicator isn't yet known-bad, not that it's proven safe - and human judgment or further analysis is still required.$q$),
('cy-q-219','cysa',1,'1.3','mcq',2,$q$An analyst compares this week's failed-login volume against the trailing 90-day baseline and finds it is five times higher, even though no single alert threshold was individually breached. What technique identified this?$q$,$q$["Static signature matching","Trend analysis comparing current data against an established baseline over time","A single-packet capture","A vulnerability scan"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Trend analysis looks at data over time relative to a baseline, catching gradual or aggregate deviations that no single event or threshold-based alert would trigger on its own.$q$),
('cy-q-220','cysa',1,'1.3','ordering',2,$q$Order the typical malware-triage steps from FIRST to LAST when an unknown suspicious file is received.$q$,$q$["Compute the file's hash and check it against known-malware databases","Perform static analysis (strings, headers) if the hash is unknown","Detonate the file in an isolated sandbox to observe its behavior","Analyze memory/network artifacts generated during detonation for new indicators"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1,2,3]$q$::jsonb,$q$Efficient triage starts cheap and gets progressively more resource-intensive: a hash lookup instantly resolves known files, static analysis of an unknown file is fast and safe, dynamic sandbox detonation reveals actual behavior when static analysis isn't conclusive, and analyzing the artifacts generated during detonation extracts new indicators for detection.$q$),
('cy-q-221','cysa',1,'1.3','mcq',3,$q$A SOC needs to retain months of network visibility for hunting but cannot afford the storage required for full packet capture at that retention period. Which approach best fits, and what do they give up?$q$,$q$["Full packet capture retained indefinitely, accepting the storage cost","Structured connection-log tools that retain rich metadata at a fraction of the storage cost, giving up the original payload content for deep inspection","Disabling network monitoring entirely to save cost","Only capturing traffic during business hours"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Connection-log/NTA tools record detailed metadata about every connection at a small fraction of full-PCAP storage cost, making long retention practical. The trade-off is that the original payload isn't retained, so deep content inspection of old traffic isn't possible without full PCAP.$q$),
('cy-q-222','cysa',1,'1.3','multi',3,$q$An analyst receives an unknown binary of uncertain origin and wants to characterize it without risking production systems. Select the TWO techniques that are appropriate FIRST steps. (Choose two.)$q$,$q$["Hash it and check reputation/threat-intel sources for a known match","Run static analysis (strings, headers) on the file without executing it","Immediately execute it on a production workstation to see what happens","Email the file to end users to see if it triggers antivirus"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1]$q$::jsonb,$q$Safe initial triage never executes an unknown file on a live system. Hashing plus reputation lookups and static analysis both characterize the file without any execution risk, and are the appropriate first steps before any sandbox detonation is even considered.$q$),
('cy-q-223','cysa',1,'1.4','mcq',1,$q$Which characteristic BEST distinguishes an Advanced Persistent Threat (APT) from an opportunistic smash-and-grab attacker?$q$,$q$["APTs only use unpatched zero-days and nothing else","APTs are well-resourced actors focused on stealthy, long-term access to achieve a sustained strategic objective","APTs always announce their presence publicly","APTs never target specific organizations"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$The defining trait of an APT is persistence and resourcing in service of a long-term goal - maintaining quiet access over time - rather than a single quick payoff. APTs use a wide range of techniques, not only zero-days, and specifically target chosen organizations.$q$),
('cy-q-224','cysa',1,'1.4','mcq',2,$q$A ransomware crew encrypts a hospital's systems and demands payment, while a separate group defaces a company's website to protest its environmental record. How do these two actors' primary motivations differ?$q$,$q$["Both are motivated purely by ideology","The ransomware crew is financially motivated; the defacement group is ideologically motivated","Both are motivated purely by financial gain","Motivation cannot be inferred from actor behavior"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Organized crime actors like ransomware crews are typically financially motivated, seeking direct payment or resale of stolen data, while hacktivists act on ideological or political goals - here, protesting a cause rather than extracting money.$q$),
('cy-q-225','cysa',1,'1.4','mcq',1,$q$A partner organization shares a threat report labeled TLP:RED during a live incident briefing. What does this label require of the recipients?$q$,$q$["They may post it publicly on social media","They may only share it with the specific individuals named as recipients, with no further distribution","They may forward it to anyone in their own organization","They may forward it to any other partner organization"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$TLP:RED is the most restrictive designation - it limits the information strictly to the named individuals in the room or on the call, with no further sharing at all, reflecting the sensitivity of live-incident details.$q$),
('cy-q-226','cysa',1,'1.4','mcq',2,$q$A vendor threat report marked TLP:AMBER is received by a SOC analyst. Which action is consistent with this designation?$q$,$q$["Publishing it on the company's public blog","Sharing it with colleagues elsewhere in the same organization on a need-to-know basis, but not outside it","Posting it to a public threat-sharing forum","Ignoring the designation since it only applies to government agencies"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$TLP:AMBER permits sharing within the recipient's own organization (and sometimes named partners) on a need-to-know basis, but explicitly prohibits wider or public distribution.$q$),
('cy-q-227','cysa',1,'1.4','mcq',1,$q$A regional hospital network joins a healthcare-sector information-sharing community to receive and contribute threat intelligence relevant to healthcare organizations. What is this type of community called?$q$,$q$["A CSIRT","An ISAC (Information Sharing and Analysis Center)","A SOAR platform","A CVE database"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$ISACs are sector-specific communities - financial services, healthcare, energy, and others - where member organizations exchange threat intelligence and best practices tailored to threats facing their particular industry.$q$),
('cy-q-228','cysa',1,'1.4','mcq',2,$q$A threat-intel team wants an open-source platform to store, correlate, and automatically share indicators across their SIEM and partner organizations. Which tool fits this need?$q$,$q$["MISP (Malware Information Sharing Platform)","A CVSS calculator","A WAF","An asset inventory spreadsheet"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$MISP is purpose-built as an open-source platform for storing, correlating, and sharing structured threat indicators and events, commonly integrated with detection tooling to feed indicators automatically.$q$),
('cy-q-229','cysa',1,'1.4','mcq',3,$q$A threat report is rated "B-2" using the Admiralty (NATO) intelligence grading system. What does this rating communicate?$q$,$q$["The report is worthless and should be discarded","The source has a strong track record of reliability (B), while this specific piece of information is probably true but not yet confirmed (2)","The information is confirmed by multiple independent sources","The rating only reflects how recently the report was published"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$The Admiralty system rates source reliability (A-F) and information credibility (1-6) independently. A "B-2" rating means a generally reliable source is reporting something judged probably true but not yet confirmed - the two grades are deliberately separate because a reliable source can still pass along unverified information.$q$),
('cy-q-230','cysa',1,'1.4','mcq',2,$q$A report detailing a specific adversary group's planned attack window against a named target, based on intercepted planning chatter, is an example of which threat intelligence tier?$q$,$q$["Strategic intelligence","Operational intelligence","Purely technical intelligence with no context","There is no such tier"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Operational intelligence covers details of a specific, often imminent campaign or planned attack - who, when, and against what target - distinct from tactical intelligence (general TTPs) or strategic intelligence (broad landscape and motivations).$q$),
('cy-q-231','cysa',1,'1.4','matching',3,$q$Match each threat intelligence tier to the example that best represents it.$q$,$q${"left":["Strategic","Tactical","Operational","Technical"],"right":["A report on how nation-state activity against the sector is trending this year, for board-level planning","A description of a group's typical TTPs, like using WMI for lateral movement","Details of a specific planned campaign against a named target next week","A raw IP address and file hash flagged as malicious, ready for SIEM ingestion"]}$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1,2,3]$q$::jsonb,$q$The four tiers serve different consumers and timeframes: strategic intelligence informs long-range, executive-level decisions; tactical intelligence describes general adversary behavior; operational intelligence covers specific, imminent campaigns; and technical intelligence is narrow, machine-readable data fed straight into detection tools.$q$),
('cy-q-232','cysa',1,'1.4','ordering',2,$q$Order the Hunting Maturity Model levels from LEAST mature to MOST mature.$q$,$q$["Relies entirely on automated alerts, with no real hunting performed","Incorporates some threat intelligence but hunts are ad hoc","Follows procedural, repeatable hunting processes created by others","Creates its own advanced hunting procedures and automates most of them into permanent detections"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1,2,3]$q$::jsonb,$q$The Hunting Maturity Model progresses from relying on automated alerting alone, through incorporating intelligence and following repeatable procedures, up to the highest level, where an organization develops its own advanced hunts and automates what it learns back into the detection stack.$q$),
('cy-q-233','cysa',1,'1.4','mcq',2,$q$Before investing further in threat intelligence collection, a CISO asks the team to first identify which systems and data would cause the most damage to the business if compromised. What is this exercise called?$q$,$q$["A penetration test","Crown jewel analysis","A CVSS scoring exercise","A tabletop exercise"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Crown jewel analysis identifies an organization's most critical assets and processes so that intelligence collection, monitoring, and hunting effort can be focused where compromise would cause the greatest impact, rather than spread evenly.$q$),
('cy-q-234','cysa',1,'1.4','mcq',3,$q$A hunt team reads a fresh vendor report describing a specific APT group's new lateral-movement technique and immediately searches their own environment for that exact behavior. Which hunting approach does this represent?$q$,$q$["Situational-awareness-driven hunting","Intel-driven hunting, using external threat intelligence to guide the search","A vulnerability scan","A penetration test"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Starting a hunt directly from external threat intelligence about a specific group's TTPs is intel-driven hunting - distinct from a hunt built from the team's own original hypothesis, or one built around protecting crown-jewel assets regardless of a specific actor.$q$),
('cy-q-235','cysa',1,'1.4','mcq',3,$q$Rather than chasing a specific actor's TTPs, a hunt team decides to continuously monitor and hunt around the organization's most critical financial systems, regardless of which threat might target them. What hunting approach is this?$q$,$q$["Intel-driven hunting","Situational-awareness-driven (crown-jewel-based) hunting","IOC-driven hunting","A compliance audit"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Situational-awareness-driven hunting focuses on protecting the organization's most critical assets and business risk, independent of any single actor's known TTPs - complementary to intel-driven or hypothesis-driven approaches that start from a specific threat.$q$),
('cy-q-236','cysa',1,'1.4','mcq',2,$q$A red team deliberately replicates the exact TTPs of a named threat actor from a recent intelligence report, while the blue team observes and tunes detections in real time during the exercise. What is this exercise called?$q$,$q$["A vulnerability scan","Adversary emulation (purple teaming)","A tabletop exercise","A compliance audit"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Deliberately replicating a specific actor's known TTPs to test and tune real detection coverage, with red and blue teams collaborating during the exercise, is adversary emulation - commonly run as a purple-team engagement.$q$),
('cy-q-237','cysa',1,'1.4','mcq',1,$q$A detection engineering team wants a visual heat map showing which ATT&CK techniques they currently have detections for and which remain uncovered. Which tool fits this need?$q$,$q$["The ATT&CK Navigator","A CVSS calculator","A SIEM dashboard showing only raw alert counts","An asset inventory tool"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$The ATT&CK Navigator renders the ATT&CK matrix as an interactive heat map, letting a team visualize and track their detection coverage, and gaps, technique by technique.$q$),
('cy-q-238','cysa',1,'1.4','multi',2,$q$Select the TWO legitimate uses of dark web monitoring as a threat-intelligence source. (Choose two.)$q$,$q$["Detecting when employee credentials from a breach are being sold or discussed on criminal forums","Getting early warning that stolen company data is being advertised for sale before public disclosure","Replacing the need for any other threat-intelligence source","Guaranteeing prevention of every future breach"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1]$q$::jsonb,$q$Dark web monitoring provides real, actionable early warning - leaked credentials or stolen data appearing for sale - but it is one intelligence source among many, not a replacement for the rest of the program, and it cannot guarantee prevention of future incidents.$q$),
('cy-q-239','cysa',1,'1.4','multi',2,$q$Select the TWO practices that improve the quality of an organization's threat-intelligence feeds. (Choose two.)$q$,$q$["Regularly evaluating feeds for relevance, timeliness, and accuracy, and dropping ones that no longer perform","Retaining every indicator indefinitely regardless of age, since more data is always better","Removing or deprioritizing stale indicators that no longer reflect active attacker infrastructure","Subscribing to as many feeds as possible with no review of overlap or quality"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,2]$q$::jsonb,$q$Good feed management means actively evaluating sources for relevance, timeliness, and accuracy, and retiring stale indicators that attackers have already rotated away from - unmanaged, ever-growing feeds full of decayed indicators create noise rather than value.$q$),
('cy-q-240','cysa',1,'1.5','mcq',1,$q$A script automatically blocks any hash confirmed malicious by a threat-intel feed. Separately, a platform coordinates that block, an enrichment lookup, a ticket creation, and an analyst notification as one connected workflow. Which term describes the platform's role?$q$,$q$["Automation","Orchestration - coordinating multiple tools and steps into one workflow","Vulnerability scanning","Static analysis"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$The single hash-blocking script is automation - one task performed without human input. The platform tying multiple tools and steps together into a coherent end-to-end response is orchestration, which is what SOAR platforms are primarily valued for.$q$),
('cy-q-241','cysa',1,'1.5','mcq',2,$q$One document lists the exact fixed steps to rotate a compromised API key. Another document branches based on scenario - different steps depending on whether ransomware has spread to backups or not. Which document is the playbook?$q$,$q$["The API key rotation document, since it is procedural","The branching document, since a playbook covers decision points across different possible scenarios","Neither qualifies as a playbook","Both are runbooks by definition"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$A runbook is a fixed, linear procedure for a specific routine task, like rotating a key; a playbook covers branching decision points across a broader incident type, like ransomware, guiding the responder differently depending on what's actually happening.$q$),
('cy-q-242','cysa',1,'1.5','mcq',2,$q$A SOC lead notices that different analysts handle the same type of phishing report inconsistently - some escalate immediately, others sit on it for hours. What is the MOST direct fix?$q$,$q$["Hire only senior analysts","Publish and enforce a Standard Operating Procedure defining the exact steps and escalation criteria for phishing reports","Disable the phishing reporting mailbox","Ignore the inconsistency since analysts have different styles"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$A documented SOP standardizes exactly how a given task should be handled, removing the variation that comes from relying on individual analyst judgment or experience for routine, well-understood scenarios.$q$),
('cy-q-243','cysa',1,'1.5','mcq',1,$q$A SOAR playbook automatically queries a threat-intel vendor's platform and receives a reputation score back within the workflow, with no analyst manually logging into the vendor's portal. What made this possible?$q$,$q$["A webhook the vendor pushed unprompted","An API integration letting the SOAR platform programmatically query the vendor and receive a response","A single pane of glass dashboard","A manual export/import process"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Programmatically querying another system and getting a response back within a workflow is exactly what an API integration provides - the SOAR platform calls the vendor's API directly instead of a human using its web interface.$q$),
('cy-q-244','cysa',1,'1.5','mcq',1,$q$The moment a new critical alert is created in the SIEM, the SIEM automatically pushes a notification to the SOAR platform's endpoint, instantly kicking off a response workflow with no polling delay. What mechanism enabled this instant push?$q$,$q$["A webhook","A single pane of glass","A runbook","A vulnerability scan"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$A webhook is exactly this pattern: the moment a triggering event occurs, the source system pushes data to a listening endpoint, starting a workflow instantly rather than waiting for the receiving system to poll for updates.$q$),
('cy-q-245','cysa',1,'1.5','mcq',2,$q$Analysts previously had to check a SIEM console, an EDR console, and a threat-intel portal separately during triage. After a dashboard is deployed that surfaces relevant data from all three in one screen, what benefit is MOST directly achieved?$q$,$q$["Elimination of all false positives","Reduced time lost to switching between separate tool consoles during triage","Automatic remediation of every alert","Replacement of the need for any underlying tool"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$A single pane of glass aggregates data from multiple underlying tools into one unified view, cutting the time and friction of context-switching between consoles - it doesn't eliminate false positives or replace the tools feeding it.$q$),
('cy-q-246','cysa',1,'1.5','mcq',2,$q$Before a new alert ever reaches an analyst, a pipeline automatically attaches the source IP's geolocation, reputation score, and the owning asset's business criticality. What does this represent?$q$,$q$["Manual triage","Automated data enrichment, giving triage a head start with context already attached","A vulnerability scan","A penetration test"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Automatically appending relevant context to a raw alert before an analyst even opens it is automated data enrichment - it removes several manual lookup steps from the start of every investigation.$q$),
('cy-q-247','cysa',1,'1.5','mcq',2,$q$A tool automatically parses newly published vendor threat reports and extracts IP addresses, domains, and hashes directly into the SIEM's watchlist, without an analyst manually copying them. What capability does this describe?$q$,$q$["Automated IOC extraction","Manual threat hunting","A CVSS scoring exercise","A tabletop exercise"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Automated IOC extraction parses unstructured or semi-structured reports and pulls out indicators programmatically, feeding them into detection tooling far faster than manual copy-paste.$q$),
('cy-q-248','cysa',1,'1.5','mcq',3,$q$A SOC subscribes to five separate threat-intel feeds with substantial indicator overlap and inconsistent formats. What is the efficiency benefit of automatically aggregating and deduplicating them into one normalized feed?$q$,$q$["It guarantees zero false positives","It removes redundant indicators and inconsistent formatting so detection tooling consumes one clean, actionable feed instead of five overlapping raw ones","It eliminates the need to ever add new feeds","It automatically blocks every listed indicator with no review"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Aggregating and normalizing multiple feeds removes duplicate indicators and format inconsistencies that would otherwise burden downstream tooling and analysts - it improves data quality and usability, not accuracy guarantees or blocking decisions by itself.$q$),
('cy-q-249','cysa',1,'1.5','mcq',3,$q$After a sandbox detonates a new malware sample, a pipeline automatically generates a detection signature from the observed byte patterns and pushes it to the EDR fleet, without a human writing the rule by hand. What does this pipeline demonstrate?$q$,$q$["Manual reverse engineering","Automated malware signature creation, speeding detection across the environment","A vulnerability scan","A tabletop exercise"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Automatically deriving a detection signature from sandbox results and distributing it removes the manual rule-writing bottleneck, letting detection for a new sample spread across the fleet far faster than a human-authored rule would.$q$),
('cy-q-250','cysa',1,'1.5','mcq',2,$q$A machine learning model baselines normal login behavior across the organization and only surfaces the small percentage of logins that deviate meaningfully, instead of an analyst reviewing every login event. What efficiency gain does this represent?$q$,$q$["It replaces the need for any human analyst entirely","It reduces the volume of low-value events analysts must manually review, letting them focus on genuinely unusual activity","It eliminates all false positives","It has no effect on analyst workload"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$ML-driven baselining filters an enormous volume of routine activity down to the genuinely anomalous, cutting the manual review burden - it doesn't eliminate the analyst's role or guarantee zero false positives, but it does meaningfully reduce low-value work.$q$),
('cy-q-251','cysa',1,'1.5','mcq',1,$q$A SOC manager says the team's goal is to "minimize human engagement" in ticket creation and indicator copy-pasting. What does this efficiency goal correctly mean?$q$,$q$["Remove human oversight from all response actions, including high-impact ones","Remove repetitive, low-judgment manual steps so analysts spend their time on tasks that need human judgment","Reduce the number of analysts on staff regardless of workload","Stop reviewing automated actions entirely"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$The efficiency goal targets repetitive, low-judgment busywork, not human oversight of consequential decisions. Automating ticket creation or indicator entry frees analyst time for genuine investigation and judgment calls, which still need a human.$q$),
('cy-q-252','cysa',1,'1.5','mcq',1,$q$As a SOAR playbook executes each containment step for a confirmed incident, it automatically creates a ticket, updates it with each action taken, and closes it once remediation completes. What capability does this represent?$q$,$q$["Manual documentation","SOAR case/ticket management integration, maintaining a documented record without manual logging","A vulnerability scan","A tabletop exercise"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Automatically creating and maintaining a ticket as a playbook runs is case/ticket management integration - it produces a complete, accurate record of response actions without requiring an analyst to manually document each step.$q$),
('cy-q-253','cysa',1,'1.5','mcq',3,$q$A SOC's automated containment playbook works end-to-end except for one legacy application server that has no API and requires an administrator to manually log in and disable the account. What does this represent?$q$,$q$["A successful full automation","A technology-integration challenge where the legacy system's lack of an API forces a manual step, capping the achievable automation","Evidence that automation should be abandoned entirely","A single pane of glass"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$A system with no programmatic interface can't participate in an automated workflow, so it forces a manual step into the process. That legacy gap, not a flaw in the automation design, is what limits how far the playbook can run unattended.$q$),
('cy-q-254','cysa',1,'1.5','mcq',2,$q$A SOC doubles its daily alert volume without adding analysts or improving triage automation, and analysts begin missing details and rushing investigations. What concept explains this decline in quality?$q$,$q$["Span of control being exceeded - too much workload per analyst for effective handling","A single pane of glass failure","A webhook malfunction","An API rate limit"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Span of control refers to how much an analyst can effectively manage at once; exceeding it, without more capacity or automation to compensate, predictably degrades investigation quality even if the tools themselves are working fine.$q$),
('cy-q-255','cysa',1,'1.5','mcq',2,$q$A ransomware playbook written two years ago still references an EDR product the organization replaced last year, and no one has updated it because no one was assigned to maintain it. What principle was missing from the start?$q$,$q$["Clear service ownership - a named owner responsible for keeping the playbook current","Single pane of glass design","API rate limiting","Span of control planning"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Without an assigned owner responsible for reviewing and updating a playbook as tools and environments change, it silently goes stale - exactly what happened here. Ownership is what keeps a process trustworthy over time, not just well-written at creation.$q$),
('cy-q-256','cysa',1,'1.5','mcq',2,$q$After a real incident revealed that a containment playbook's step ordering caused unnecessary downtime, the team updates the playbook to reflect what was learned before the next incident occurs. What practice does this represent?$q$,$q$["Ignoring lessons learned","A continuous-improvement feedback loop feeding lessons learned back into the process","A single pane of glass upgrade","An API integration"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Feeding lessons learned from a real incident back into updating the relevant playbook, rather than leaving it static, is exactly what a continuous-improvement feedback loop looks like - it keeps automation and procedures aligned with reality.$q$),
('cy-q-257','cysa',1,'1.5','mcq',2,$q$Leadership asks the SOC to justify continued investment in its automation program. Which metric MOST directly demonstrates its value?$q$,$q$["The total number of tools purchased","Mean time to respond (MTTR) before automation versus after, and analyst hours saved","The number of slides in the quarterly report","The size of the SOC's office space"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Concrete before/after metrics like MTTR reduction and analyst hours saved directly demonstrate automation's operational impact, giving leadership a measurable basis to justify continued investment, unlike unrelated figures like tool count or office space.$q$),
('cy-q-258','cysa',1,'1.5','multi',2,$q$Select the TWO metrics that meaningfully demonstrate the value of a new SOC automation initiative. (Choose two.)$q$,$q$["Reduction in mean time to respond (MTTR) after automation went live","Analyst hours saved on previously manual, repetitive tasks","The total number of email accounts in the organization","The color scheme of the SOC's dashboard"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1]$q$::jsonb,$q$MTTR reduction and analyst hours saved are direct, outcome-based measures of automation's impact. Organization-wide account counts and dashboard aesthetics say nothing about whether the automation initiative actually improved efficiency.$q$),
('cy-q-259','cysa',1,'1.5','multi',3,$q$Select the TWO real risks of allowing an automated playbook to take high-impact remediation actions, such as blocking an IP org-wide, with no human approval step. (Choose two.)$q$,$q$["A false positive could trigger an action that disrupts legitimate business operations","The blast radius of a bad automated decision can be large and immediate since it executes at machine speed","Automation always produces better decisions than a human would","There is no meaningful risk once a playbook is deployed"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1]$q$::jsonb,$q$Unattended high-impact automation executes exactly what it's told, instantly and at scale, so a misfire from a false positive can cause real business disruption before anyone notices. This is why approval gates or staged rollouts are recommended for consequential actions - not because automation is inherently worse than a human, but because it removes the moment where a human might catch an error.$q$),
('cy-q-260','cysa',1,'1.5','ordering',2,$q$Order the steps of an automated phishing-report triage playbook from FIRST to LAST.$q$,$q$["Ingest the user-reported email into the SOAR platform","Extract and enrich indicators, such as the sender domain, URLs, and attachment hash, automatically","Detonate any attachment or URL in a sandbox if indicators are inconclusive","Block confirmed-malicious indicators, notify the reporting user, then close the ticket"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1,2,3]$q$::jsonb,$q$A well-formed automated triage flow starts by pulling the reported email into the platform, then enriches the extracted indicators for fast context, escalates to sandbox detonation only when needed, and finishes by acting on confirmed findings and closing the loop with the user - cheapest, fastest steps first, escalating only as needed.$q$),
('cy-q-261','cysa',1,'1.5','matching',3,$q$Match each automation-related concept to its definition.$q$,$q${"left":["Runbook","Webhook","Single pane of glass","API"],"right":["A fixed, step-by-step procedure for a specific routine task","An event-driven push that instantly triggers a workflow when something happens","A unified dashboard aggregating data from multiple underlying tools","A defined interface letting one program programmatically query or control another"]}$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1,2,3]$q$::jsonb,$q$Each term plays a distinct role in SOC efficiency: a runbook is the fixed procedure itself, a webhook is the instant event-driven trigger, a single pane of glass is the unified viewing layer, and an API is the programmatic connection that makes tool-to-tool automation possible in the first place.$q$);
